---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { ISSUES_URL, REPO_CONFIGURED, REPO_URL } from '../site';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const title = 'Privacy: Krova';
const description =
  'Krova makes no network calls, has no accounts or telemetry, and never logs window titles. Here is exactly what it stores on your computer, and where.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="container container--narrow">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
      <h1>Privacy, in plain language</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Krova is an offline app. It does not send anything anywhere, so there is no data of yours for us to hold,
        sell or lose.
      </p>
    </header>

    <article class="prose" aria-label="Privacy details">
      <h2>The short version</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Krova makes <strong>no network calls</strong>.</li>
        <li>There are <strong>no accounts</strong> and <strong>no telemetry</strong>.</li>
        <li><strong>Window titles are never logged or stored.</strong></li>
        <li>Your settings and layouts stay in <strong>one local JSON file</strong> that you can read, copy or delete.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What Krova does on the network</h2>
      <p>
        Nothing. The app makes no network calls at all: no sign-in, no usage statistics, no crash reports, no update
        pings. Because it never connects out, there is no server on the other side that could receive your data.
      </p>
      <p>
        You do not have to take our word for it. The source code is open under the MIT License, and you can watch
        Krova with your operating system’s network monitor or block it in your firewall. It should behave exactly the
        same.
      </p>

      <h2>What is stored on your computer</h2>
      <p>
        Krova keeps one file, <code>config.json</code>, in your user account’s application-data folder (typically under
        <code>%APPDATA%</code> on Windows and <code>~/Library/Application Support</code> on macOS). It contains:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Settings:</strong> the activation key, highlight color and opacity, animated or instant moves, edge
          snapping on or off and its size, launch at login, enabled or disabled, and theme.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Per-monitor data:</strong> for each monitor, an identifier, a label, its layouts (zones saved as
          proportions of the screen) and which layout is active.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        To recognise a monitor when you plug it back in, Krova stores what the operating system reports about it: the
        manufacturer and product codes (and the serial number, where the system provides one), the device path, and the
        monitor’s last size and position. These are what let Krova bring a layout back for the same monitor. They stay
        in this file on your machine. If you ever share your <code>config.json</code>, for example in a bug report,
        remove those identifiers first if you would rather not share them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The file is written safely (to a temporary file, then renamed). If it ever turns out to be unreadable, Krova
        renames it to <code>config.json.bak</code> and starts again with defaults instead of discarding it.
      </p>

      <h2>What Krova does not record</h2>
      <p>
        To move a window, Krova has to look at the window you are dragging, its position and size, and at where your
        cursor is. That happens in memory, in the moment. <strong>Window titles are never logged or stored</strong>,
        and nothing about the windows you move is written to <code>config.json</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Permissions</h2>
      <p>
        On macOS, Krova asks for <strong>Accessibility</strong> permission (System Settings, Privacy &amp; Security,
        Accessibility). macOS requires it before any app can move and resize other apps’ windows. Depending on your
        macOS version it may also ask for <strong>Input Monitoring</strong> so that it can notice a window being
        dragged. Krova only watches the left mouse button and checks whether your activation key and Escape are held;
        it does not read or record what you type. On Windows no special permission is requested; windows of apps
        running as administrator are skipped.
      </p>

      <h2>Removing your data</h2>
      <p>
        Delete <code>config.json</code> (and <code>config.json.bak</code>, if present) to erase the settings and
        layouts Krova has stored. Krova keeps no copy anywhere else, because it never sends anything anywhere.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This site is a set of static pages. It sets no cookies, runs no analytics, has no forms and loads no scripts,
        fonts, images or embeds from other servers. Like any web server, whatever hosts these pages may keep ordinary
        access logs; the site itself does nothing to add to that.
      </p>
      <p>
        Downloading an installer necessarily contacts the server that hosts it (for example GitHub, if you get it from
        the releases page). That request is handled under that service’s own privacy policy, not by Krova.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        {
          REPO_CONFIGURED ? (
            <Fragment>
              If something here is unclear or looks wrong, please <a href={ISSUES_URL}>open an issue</a> in the{' '}
              <a href={REPO_URL}>project repository</a>.
            </Fragment>
          ) : (
            <Fragment>If something here is unclear or looks wrong, please open an issue in the project repository.</Fragment>
          )
        }
      </p>
      <p class="muted">Last updated: 30 October 2026. See also the <a href={url('/license/')}>license</a>.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  article {
    padding-bottom: var(--section-y);
  }
</style>
